      "line": 146,
      "title": "Local assignment does not cache the global parser",
      "body": "Assigning `parser = Parser.from_matchers(matchers)` inside `__getattr__` only creates a local variable; it does not update the module-level `parser` global, so the parser is still recreated on every attribute access. The PR does not actually fix the caching problem it claims to solve. To cache it, you must declare `global parser` and assign to it (and ideally set it via `globals()['parser']` or module attribute) so subsequent lookups hit the cached instance rather than re-triggering `__getattr__`. For example:\n\n```python\ndef __getattr__(name: str) -> Parser:\n    if name == 'parser':\n        global parser\n        ...\n        parser = Parser.from_matchers(matchers)\n        return parser\n    raise AttributeError(...)\n```\n\nNote that once the module-level global `parser` is set, `__getattr__` will no longer be invoked for that name (module `__getattr__` is only called on missing attributes), which is what actually achieves the caching."
    }
  ]
}

