      "title": "Padding with zero degrades signal quality and normalization",
      "body": "The root cause is that np.fft.irfft() defaults to an even output length (2*(n_freq-1)). For odd-length signals the correct fix is to pass the explicit output length to irfft, i.e. `np.fft.irfft(white_noise_shaped, n=samples)`. This reconstructs the signal at the exact requested length using proper inverse FFT semantics, rather than appending a spurious zero sample. Padding with a zero introduces an artificial discontinuity at the end of the noise and slightly alters the signal's statistical/energy properties (which matter if the pink noise is later normalized to a target RMS/gain). Please generate the noise directly at the correct length via the `n` parameter and remove the pad/truncate branching."
    },
