      "body": "While the first IP in X-Forwarded-For is semantically the original client per MDN, in a proxy trust context the last IP is the one appended by the immediately upstream (trusted) proxy. The first IP is fully client-controlled and can be spoofed by anyone sending an arbitrary X-Forwarded-For header. Blindly trusting the first IP allows clients to forge their apparent source address, which can defeat IP allowlists, rate limiting, and logging integrity. The middleware also has a `trusted_hosts` mechanism that is intended to work by walking from the rightmost (nearest) proxy inward; changing to the leftmost IP without respecting the number of trusted proxy hops breaks that model. Please reconsider this change: the correct fix is to skip a configurable number of trusted proxies from the right, not to unconditionally take the leftmost entry."
