      "title": "Single-tuple case skips state.append and breaks interpreter stage",
      "body": "In the len(tuples) == 1 branch you push tuples[0] directly without calling state.append(inst, ...). BUILD_TUPLE_UNPACK is a real bytecode instruction that must be recorded so the later interpreter/lowering stage can process it (op_BUILD_TUPLE_UNPACK expects a corresponding entry with tuples/temps). By not appending, the instruction has no recorded effect for the interpreter, and re-pushing the input tuple's variable can also alias/reuse a var that the block dataflow doesn't expect. More importantly, `(*x,)` must still produce a NEW tuple (unpack+rebuild), not simply alias x — semantically pushing the source tuple is not equivalent. The correct fix is to keep appending the instruction and always create a fresh temp (e.g. temps = [state.make_temp() for _ in range(max(len(tuples), 1))] or a single result temp) and push that temp, rather than short-circuiting the single-tuple case."
