      "title": "New glob pattern breaks matching when basename has no trailing suffix part",
      "body": "Changing `{basename}*` to `{basename}_*` requires a literal underscore immediately after the basename. This breaks legitimate cases where the file's suffix/extension follows the last entity without a further underscore-separated component being part of the basename. For example, a BIDSPath with a full basename like `sub-01_task-rest_eeg` would previously match `sub-01_task-rest_eeg.fif` via `sub-01_task-rest_eeg*`, but now `sub-01_task-rest_eeg_*` will NOT match `sub-01_task-rest_eeg.fif` (no underscore before `.fif`). More generally, when the constructed basename already ends at the point where only an extension follows (e.g. matching by suffix, or matching a directory-level entity path), `_*` will fail. This regresses common lookups. Consider a pattern that requires either an underscore separator OR end-of-entity boundary, e.g. searching `{basename}_*` AND `{basename}.*`, or building the glob from the known extension/suffix, or filtering candidates by parsing entities rather than relying on the glob boundary."
    },
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "category": "test",
      "path": "mne_bids/tests/test_path.py",
      "line": 1651,
      "title": "Test does not cover the regressed case (basename ending in suffix)",
      "body": "The added test only exercises files of the form `sub-XX_run-Y_eeg.fif`, where the matched entity `run` is always followed by another underscore-separated component (`_eeg`). It never tests a BIDSPath whose basename ends at the final entity right before the extension/suffix (e.g. constructing a path where the glob would need to match `...eeg.fif` directly). As a result, the suite will pass even though the `_*` change breaks such cases. Add a test that constructs a BIDSPath resolving to a file where the entity being matched is the last basename component before the extension, to catch the regression described above."
